TypeScript's conditional types is a feature that allows the compiler to compute and assign types based on input types at compile-time. This mechanism lets developers create versatile type definitions, tailoring them to specific use cases. For instance, when defining a 'getElementType' utility function which infers element types from React components, one might employ conditional types to accurately derive either the native HTML element type or the custom component class: 

```typescript
interface IProps { component: React.ComponentType<any>; }
type ElementType<P = {}> = P extends { component: infer C } ? C : never;
const getElementType: <T>(props: T & IProps) => ElementType<T> = (props) => props.component;
```
Here, the 'ElementType' conditional type computes the underlying element or component type of a given React component.

C++ is a general-purpose programming language that was developed by Bjarne Stroustrup at Bell Labs in the early 1980s. It is an extension of the C programming language, with enhancements such as classes and objects, which provide support for object-oriented programming. The 'Hello, World!' program is a classic and time-honored tradition in computer programming, serving as a gentle introduction to a programming language's most basic syntax and structures. In C++, a Hello, World program might look like this: `#include <iostream>
int main() {
std::cout << "Hello, World!\n";
return 0;
}` This simple program uses the iostream library to output the text 'Hello, World!' to the console.
